New: The visco-plastic material model now supports differential dynamic
friction weakening. The high-strain-rate friction angle can depend on
whether the projected surface-velocity divergence indicates a convergent,
neutral/transform, or divergent tectonic regime.
<br>
(Michaël Pons, 2026/07/28)

New: The visco-plastic material model now provides the dominant deformation
mechanism selected during rheology evaluation as a named additional output.
The output contains categorical values for diffusion creep, dislocation creep,
plastic yielding, Peierls creep, the maximum-viscosity limiter, grain-boundary
sliding, and Frank-Kamenetskii rheology.
<br>
(Michael Pons, 2026/07/28)

Fixed: When mesh deformation is enabled, the adiabatic pressure used by the
visco-plastic material model for plastic yielding and viscous creep is now
evaluated using depth below the current deformed surface. This prevents surface
depressions such as trenches from becoming artificially strong.
<br>
(Michaël Pons, 2026/07/28)
